Added the authorization server's admin API. `@identity-providers` manages who this site lets people log in *with*; `@identity-clients` and `@identity-keys` are the other direction — who may log in *to* it, and what this server signs with. Full CRUD on client registrations plus a `rotate-secret` action, and a signing-ring description plus a `rotate` action. Everything needs `Manage portal` and everything is bound to the `[server]` browser layer, so a site that never switched the authorization server on does not publish an API for managing one. The services are registered for View and guard internally, so a caller without the permission gets a JSON refusal rather than the login page an anonymous request to a protected view now produces.

Client secrets behave the opposite way to provider secrets, deliberately. A provider's is masked and can be echoed back unchanged, because this package is the client there and has to keep sending it. Here this package is the server: the secret is scrypt-hashed at rest (S8) and nothing needs the plaintext again, so it appears in exactly one response — the one that mints it — and cannot be read back. The response says so in as many words, because an operator who does not know that loses it and files a bug. The stored hash never leaves at all: it is not a secret, but publishing one invites an offline attack on a value the site owner cannot rotate without breaking the client.

`client_id` and `auth_method` are not editable — renaming would orphan every token minted for a client, and turning a confidential client public would leave a stored hash nothing checks. A PATCH naming any other unknown field is refused rather than ignored, since silently dropping one is how an operator comes to believe they changed something they did not. A client registered for the authorization code grant with no redirect URI is refused at registration rather than at `/authorize` much later, where the failure reads like a client bug.

`GET @identity-keys` returns key ids and which one is active — never key material, not even public halves, which are already at `@@oauth-jwks` and would otherwise exist in two places to drift apart. Rotation keeps the previous keys so tokens issued before it keep verifying by `kid`, and the response reports the ring bound, because rotating past it within one access-token lifetime does invalidate tokens still in flight and that should be visible rather than discovered. @ericof
